    historyContent: {
      origins: "Fifty-Five evolved from 'Maw' (16th century) and 'Spoil Five' (19th century). While Twenty-Five is the foundational version, Fifty-Five added the bidding layer to increase the strategic depth and scoring potential.",
      originCountries: [
        'Ireland',
      ],
      timeline: [
        '1500s: The game of Maw is recorded in the British Isles.',
        '1800s: Spoil Five becomes the definitive Irish pub game.',
        '1900s: Fifty-Five emerges as a popular tournament and highndstakes variation.',
        'Present: Remains a staple of rural life in West Ireland and Atlantic coastal communities.',
      ],
      evolution: "The game's unusual rankings (e.g., Five being high) are thought to be remnants of very old European game structures that predated the standardized Ace-High logic of Bridge and Whist.",
      cultural: "An integral part of Irish sociondcultural history; often played during 'stations' (house masses) or as a communal activity in agricultural circles.",
    },
